=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough live investigation of R12, R11, R13, and PC12 finds no active fault consistent with the reported symptom. PC12 successfully pinged its default gateway (120.1.1.1 on R12's eth1, GG segment) 20 consecutive times with 0% packet loss and a ~0.36 ms average RTT, disproving the "gateway drops out" assertion. R12's eth1 interface is persistently UP with zero RX/TX errors or drops. The EIGRP AS 100 adjacency between R12 and R11 (over the EE/112.x.x.x segment) has been continuously stable since lab start — the FRR log records a single clean formation event at 14:43:36 with zero retransmissions and no subsequent bounces. The EIGRP topology is entirely Passive on both R12 and R11, with no active queries or SIA conditions. Hello/hold timers are symmetric at 5 s/15 s on all EIGRP interfaces. No periodic scripts, watchdog processes, or cron jobs capable of causing interface or FRR disruption were found on R12 or R11. The only real configuration anomaly is that R12's startup script enables `ospfd=yes` in /etc/frr/daemons even though frr.conf contains no OSPF configuration; ospfd has been idle (PID 69, running since boot, "OSPF is not enabled in vrf default") and presents no operational risk at this time. The suspected R12 LAN flap and EIGRP neighbor bounce are both false: neither condition exists in the live lab. The gateway is stable, EIGRP is healthy, and the helpdesk ticket describes a symptom that is not present in this topology.
=== END ===